<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guánica</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guánica is a town on the south-western coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a>. On 25 July 1898, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war">Spanish–American War</a>, US troops under General Nelson Miles landed there to begin the invasion of Puerto Rico. The area is known for its dry forest, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> biosphere reserve.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guanica.jpg" alt="Guánica" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Les &amp; Omy · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Atardecer_en_San_Jacinto,_Guanica_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Guánica lies in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a> on the Caribbean Sea, between Lajas to the west and Yauco to the east, about 20 miles (30 kilometres) west of Ponce. The town stands at the head of a deep harbour bordered by rugged hills, barely a quarter-mile wide but about two miles long, which has been likened to a tropical fjord. The ridge of the Cordillera Central keeps rain from the area: the north-east of the island receives over 100 inches a year, Guánica less than 30. Its dry forest reserve was designated an international Biosphere Reserve in 1981.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-ponce-de-leon">Juan Ponce de León</a> landed in the harbour on 12 August 1508 and founded a town called Guaynía, from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> word popularly said to mean here is a place with water. Considered the first capital of the island, then named Isla de San Juan Bautista, it was destroyed in the indigenous uprising of 1511, and Europeans left the area for some years while San Juan became the capital. The municipal coat of arms recalls the Taíno chief Agüeybaná, whose territory lay in this region.</p><p>In the landing of 1898 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucester">Gloucester</a> was the first ship to anchor in the bay. Twenty-eight sailors and Marines under lieutenants H. P. Huse and Wood came ashore on rafts, replaced the Spanish flag with the American one and set up a machine gun post. Lt. Méndez López and his men of the Puerto Rican militia attacked them in the first land skirmish of the campaign; López and three of his men were wounded, and the unit fell back to Yauco. July 25 was long kept in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a> as Occupation Day, later renamed Constitution Day.</p><p>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 1899 by the United States Department of War counted 2,700 people in Guánica, which was at first a barrio of Yauco and became a separate municipality on 13 March 1914, with Víctor Ángel Sallaberry Safini as its first mayor. Its economy was long dominated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantations of Central Guánica.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The town centre is a compact grid of twelve streets around the main square, where the Catholic church and city hall stand. The municipality had 13,787 inhabitants in 2020. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hurricane-maria">Hurricane Maria</a> damaged or destroyed 951 homes there on 20 September 2017, and earthquakes of magnitude 5.8 and 6.4 struck on 6 and 7 January 2020.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Guánica, Puerto Rico” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>American troops landed at <b>Guánica</b> in July 1898 during the Spanish-American War, months after Spain had granted Puerto Rico an autonomous charter.</blockquote><a href="/state/puerto-rico-united-states">Puerto Rico (United States)</a></li></ul></section>
